        {
            "name": "Permutation & Combination",
            "content": "Permutation and Combination are fundamental counting principles used to determine the number of possible arrangements and selections. Permutation deals with the arrangement of objects where order matters, while Combination deals with selection where order does not matter. This topic covers factorial notation, nPr and nCr formulas, circular permutations, and problems involving restrictions and repetitions. These concepts form the basis for probability and are widely tested in aptitude exams.",
            "order_index": 0,
            "videos": []
        },
        {
            "name": "Probability",
            "content": "Probability is the measure of the likelihood of an event occurring, expressed as a number between 0 and 1. This topic covers basic probability, conditional probability, independent and dependent events, Bayes' theorem, and problems involving coins, dice, cards, and balls. A strong grasp of probability is essential for data science, statistics, and competitive aptitude exams.",
            "order_index": 1,
            "videos": []
        },
        {
            "name": "Data Interpretation",
            "content": "Data Interpretation involves analyzing and drawing conclusions from data presented in various formats such as tables, bar graphs, pie charts, line graphs, and caselets. This topic tests the ability to quickly extract relevant information, perform calculations, and make comparisons. Data Interpretation is a high-weightage section in most competitive exams and requires proficiency in percentages, ratios, and averages.",
            "order_index": 2,
            "videos": []
        },
        {
            "name": "Algebra",
            "content": "Algebra deals with mathematical symbols and the rules for manipulating them to solve equations and inequalities. This topic covers linear equations, quadratic equations, polynomials, inequalities, modular arithmetic, and systems of equations. Algebraic problem-solving skills are critical for aptitude tests and form the backbone of many quantitative reasoning problems.",
            "order_index": 3,
            "videos": []
        },
        {
            "name": "Geometry",
            "content": "Geometry is the study of shapes, sizes, positions, and properties of space. This topic covers lines, angles, triangles, circles, quadrilaterals, polygons, coordinate geometry, and theorems related to congruence and similarity. Understanding geometric properties and relationships is essential for solving spatial reasoning and measurement problems in aptitude exams.",
            "order_index": 4,
            "videos": []
        },
        {
            "name": "Mensuration",
            "content": "Mensuration deals with the measurement of geometric figures including their area, perimeter, surface area, and volume. This topic covers 2D shapes (rectangles, circles, triangles, trapeziums) and 3D solids (cubes, cuboids, cylinders, cones, spheres). Mensuration problems are commonly asked in aptitude exams and require applying formulas to real-world measurement scenarios.",
            "order_index": 5,
            "videos": []
        },
        {
            "name": "Logarithms",
            "content": "Logarithms are the inverse operations of exponentiation and are used to solve equations involving exponential expressions. This topic covers logarithmic laws (product, quotient, power rules), change of base formula, natural and common logarithms, and applications in simplifying complex calculations. Logarithms are frequently tested in competitive exams and are essential for understanding exponential growth and scientific computations.",
            "order_index": 6,
            "videos": []
        },
        {
            "name": "Progressions",
            "content": "Progressions are sequences of numbers that follow a specific pattern. This topic covers Arithmetic Progression (AP), Geometric Progression (GP), Harmonic Progression (HP), and special series. It includes finding the nth term, sum of n terms, and properties of each type of progression. Progressions are important for solving pattern-based and series-related problems in aptitude exams.",
            "order_index": 7,
            "videos": []
        },
        {
            "name": "Functions & Graphs",
            "content": "Functions define the relationship between input and output values, and graphs provide their visual representation. This topic covers types of functions (linear, quadratic, exponential, logarithmic), domain and range, composite functions, inverse functions, and graph transformations. Understanding functions and their graphical behavior is essential for analytical reasoning and higher-level quantitative problems.",
            "order_index": 8,
            "videos": []
        },
        {
            "name": "Advanced Arithmetic",
            "content": "Advanced Arithmetic extends basic arithmetic concepts to more complex problem-solving scenarios. This topic covers partnership problems, work and wages, races and games, clocks and calendars, surds and indices, and simplification of complex expressions. These problems require combining multiple arithmetic concepts and are frequently encountered in competitive aptitude examinations.",
            "order_index": 9,
            "videos": []
        }
    ],
